          "title": "You'll want to know: praeda, praedae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "praeda, praedae — \"loot, booty; prey\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latrōnēs praedam in spēluncā posuērunt. The bandits put the loot in a cave."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine. Also the prey of an animal. English prey comes from it through French, and predator from its verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "praeda is explained by Lewis & Short as a short form of an older praehenda, \"a thing seized\", from prehendō, \"to seize\". English prey comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
